  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Left gray casing with 'Hydrogen' inlet and 'Unused hydrogen' outlet",
    "Right gray casing with 'Oxygen' inlet and 'Water' outlet",
    "Anode plate (purple, left)",
    "Proton exchange membrane (blue, center)",
    "Cathode plate (purple, right)",
    "External circuit wire (top, black)",
    "Resistor / Electric power symbol (yellow zigzag, top)",
    "H2 molecules (paired white spheres)",
    "O2 molecules (paired red spheres)",
    "H2O molecules (one red sphere, two white spheres)",
    "Protons / H+ ions (white spheres with '+')",
    "Electrons / e- (yellow circles with '-')",
    "Mass flow arrows (white, indicating particle paths)",
    "Electron flow arrows (black, on external circuit)",
    "Label: 'Hydrogen'",
    "Label: 'Unused hydrogen'",
    "Label: 'Anode'",
    "Label: 'Proton exchange membrane'",
    "Label: 'Cathode'",
    "Label: 'Oxygen'",
    "Label: 'Water'",
    "Label: 'Electric power'"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "fuelCellCycle",
      "type": "button",
      "label": "Step through reaction",
      "states": [
        "Idle",
        "Oxidation at Anode",
        "Current & Ion Flow",
        "Reduction at Cathode"
      ],
      "default": "Idle",
      "teaches": "The overall reaction is broken into distinct steps: hydrogen splits into protons and electrons, electrons do work in the circuit while protons cross the membrane, and finally they recombine with oxygen."
    },
    {
      "id": "h2FeedRate",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Hydrogen feed rate",
      "range": [1, 5, 1],
      "default": 3,
      "teaches": "Increasing the reactant fuel rate proportionally increases the electron flow (electrical current) and the rate of water production."
    },
    {
      "id": "viewMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Display mode",
      "options": [
        "Fully labeled",
        "Clean flow schematic"
      ],
      "default": "Fully labeled",
      "teaches": "Removing the structural labels helps emphasize the continuous, simultaneous flow of mass and charge through the system."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The Fuel Cell Structure",
      "narration": "A fuel cell consists of two electrodes (anode and cathode) separated by a proton exchange membrane. Reactants flow in, and products flow out.",
      "state": {
        "fuelCellCycle": "Idle",
        "h2FeedRate": 3,
        "viewMode": "Fully labeled"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Oxidation at the Anode",
      "narration": "Hydrogen gas enters the left side and is oxidized at the anode, splitting into positively charged protons (H+) and negatively charged electrons (e-).",
      "state": {
        "fuelCellCycle": "Oxidation at Anode",
        "h2FeedRate": 3,
        "viewMode": "Fully labeled"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Generating Current",
      "narration": "The central membrane only allows protons to pass. To reach the other side, electrons are forced to travel through the external circuit, generating electric power.",
      "state": {
        "fuelCellCycle": "Current & Ion Flow",
        "h2FeedRate": 3,
        "viewMode": "Fully labeled"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Reduction and Water Formation",
      "narration": "At the cathode, the arriving protons and electrons combine with incoming oxygen gas to produce water, the only byproduct of this process.",
      "state": {
        "fuelCellCycle": "Reduction at Cathode",
        "h2FeedRate": 3,
        "viewMode": "Fully labeled"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Adjusting Power Output",
      "narration": "Increasing the supply of hydrogen fuel directly increases both the electrical current generated and the amount of water produced.",
      "state": {
        "fuelCellCycle": "Reduction at Cathode",
        "h2FeedRate": 5,
        "viewMode": "Fully labeled"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Continuous Flow",
      "narration": "In operation, all these steps happen simultaneously, creating a continuous conversion of chemical energy into electrical energy.",
      "state": {
        "fuelCellCycle": "Reduction at Cathode",
        "h2FeedRate": 5,
        "viewMode": "Clean flow schematic"
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "By physically separating the electron path (circuit) from the proton path (membrane), a fuel cell forces a spontaneous chemical reaction to do useful electrical work.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Create a symmetrical layout with a central vertical sandwich: purple Anode, blue Proton exchange membrane, purple Cathode. The left gray casing routes H2 (paired white spheres) to the anode, with a lower exhaust. The right gray casing routes O2 (paired red spheres) to the cathode, with a lower exhaust for H2O (one red, two white spheres). H+ tokens (white with '+') move left to right through the blue center. e- tokens (yellow with '-') move up from the anode, across a top wire with a yellow resistor, and down to the cathode. In 'Clean flow schematic' mode, hide all text annotations so only the structural blocks, particles, and flow arrows remain."
